WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.112
NumPy indexing will feel familiar because it follows many of the same ideas as Python lists.

00:00:06.112 --> 00:00:09.227
Index zero selects the first element.

00:00:09.227 --> 00:00:12.732
Index negative one selects the last element.

00:00:12.732 --> 00:00:17.352
A slice such as colon three selects the first three values.

00:00:17.352 --> 00:00:21.412
A slice with a step of two selects every other value.

00:00:21.412 --> 00:00:24.482
Click Run and observe all four examples.

00:00:24.482 --> 00:00:30.997
These operations are simple with a one-dimensional array, but they form the foundation for selecting rows,

00:00:30.997 --> 00:00:34.852
columns, and blocks from multidimensional arrays later.

00:00:34.852 --> 00:00:38.512
Remember that Python uses zero-based indexing.

00:00:38.512 --> 00:00:42.527
The first item is at position zero, not position one.

00:00:42.527 --> 00:00:48.967
In real analysis, slicing can help us examine a time window, choose a subset of observations,

00:00:48.967 --> 00:00:51.752
or inspect part of a numerical sequence.

00:00:51.752 --> 00:00:58.557
The syntax is compact, so when you read an unfamiliar slice, slow down and identify the start position,

00:00:58.557 --> 00:01:00.508
stop position, and step.
